Notice that the sixth term (highlighted in red) has a nilpotent product:<tbc>

\(U_6V_6W_6=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 1 \\
1 & -1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 1 \\
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right).\) (2.4)

<tbc>Hence, we can follow the proof of Theorem 2.1 to construct symbolic basis transformations<tbc>

\(R=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon & 0 \\
0 & \varepsilon^3
\end{array}\right),S=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon & 0 \\
0 & \varepsilon^4
\end{array}\right),\text{and}T=\left(\begin{array}{ c c c }
2 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 0 & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{ c c c }
\varepsilon & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \varepsilon^2 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & \varepsilon^5
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{ c c c }
2 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 0 & 1
\end{array}\right)^{-1},\) (2.5)

<tbc>which, when applied to each term in (2.3), yield

\(\mathcal{M}_{\langle 2,2,3\rangle}=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & \varepsilon^3 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2}+\varepsilon^{-3} & 2\varepsilon^{-2}-\varepsilon^{-3}
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2\varepsilon^{-1}-2 & 0 \\
2\varepsilon^{-1}-1 & 0 \\
\varepsilon^{-4}-1 & 0
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
\varepsilon^{-2} & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^4-\varepsilon+1 & -\varepsilon^4+2\varepsilon-1 & -\varepsilon^4 \\
\varepsilon-\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2-\varepsilon^{-1} & 2\varepsilon^2-\varepsilon \\
1-\varepsilon^{-1} & \varepsilon^2-\varepsilon \\
1-\varepsilon^{-4} & \varepsilon^2-\varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & -\varepsilon^3 \\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{-3} & \varepsilon-\varepsilon^{-3}
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-4} & -\varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^4-\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4+2\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4 \\
\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{-2}-2\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon-2\varepsilon^{-2}+2\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2\varepsilon^{-1}-2 & \varepsilon \\
2\varepsilon^{-1}-1 & \varepsilon \\
\varepsilon^{-4}-1 & \varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & -\varepsilon^3 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2}+2\varepsilon^{-3} & 2\varepsilon^{-2}-2\varepsilon^{-3}
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^{-1}-2 & 0 \\
\varepsilon^{-1}-1 & 0 \\
\varepsilon^{-4}-1 & \varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
0 & \varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^4-\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4+2\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4 \\
\varepsilon & -\varepsilon & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 2\varepsilon^2-\varepsilon \\
0 & \varepsilon^2-\varepsilon \\
0 & \varepsilon^2
\end{array}\right)\) (2.6)

\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
\varepsilon^{-2} & \varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^4-\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4+2\varepsilon & -\varepsilon^4 \\
\varepsilon-\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2\varepsilon^{-1}-2 & -2\varepsilon^2+2\varepsilon \\
2\varepsilon^{-1}-1 & -\varepsilon^2+2\varepsilon \\
\varepsilon^{-4}-1 & \varepsilon^{-2}-\varepsilon^2
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1-\varepsilon & 2\varepsilon-1 \\
\varepsilon^{-2}-\varepsilon^{-3} & -2\varepsilon^{-2}+\varepsilon^{-3}
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2-\varepsilon^{-1} & -\varepsilon \\
1-\varepsilon^{-1} & -\varepsilon \\
1-\varepsilon^{-4} & -\varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & -\varepsilon^3 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & \varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
\varepsilon-\varepsilon^{-2}+\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon+2\varepsilon^{-2}-\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 \\
0 & -\varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 0 \\
-\varepsilon^{-2} & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^4 & -\varepsilon^4 & -\varepsilon^4 \\
\varepsilon-2\varepsilon^{-2}+2\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon+4\varepsilon^{-2}-2\varepsilon^{-3} & -\varepsilon
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
2-\varepsilon^{-1} & 0 \\
1-\varepsilon^{-1} & 0 \\
1-\varepsilon^{-4} & 0
\end{array}\right)\)<br>
\(+\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 0 \\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1-\varepsilon^4 & \varepsilon^4-1 & \varepsilon^4 \\
-\varepsilon+\varepsilon^{-3} & \varepsilon-\varepsilon^{-3}
\end{array}\right)\otimes\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^{-1} & \varepsilon \\
\varepsilon^{-1} & \varepsilon \\
\varepsilon^{-4} & \varepsilon^{-2}
\end{array}\right).\)

As expected, the sixth term is now \(O(\varepsilon^3)\) and can be deleted to obtain a \(\langle 2,2,3;10\rangle\) APA algorithm.

Note that the choice of _R_, _S_, and _T_ is not unique. Moreover, it is possible to construct suitable basis transformations without relying on the proof of Theorem 2.1. As an example, we could take instead<tbc>

\(R=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 0 \\
0 & \varepsilon^{-1}
\end{array}\right),S=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^{-3} & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right),T=\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
1 & 2 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 1 \\
1 & 1 & 0
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{ c c }
\varepsilon^{-2} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{array}\right).\) (2.7)
